## Description

`InMemoryGraphStore.findNodesByLabel` matches any node whose label *contains* the search term as a case-insensitive substring, rather than matching the label exactly:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/graph-store.ts:89-94
findNodesByLabel(label: string): GraphNode[] {
  const normalized = label.toLowerCase();
  return Array.from(this.nodes.values()).filter((n) =>
    n.label.toLowerCase().includes(normalized)
  );
}
```

During graph construction, `MemoryManager.extractAndStoreGraph` uses this method to decide whether an extracted entity already exists, and reuses the first hit instead of creating a new node:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/memory-manager.ts:183-194
const existing = this.graphStore.findNodesByLabel(entity.label);
if (existing.length > 0) {
  nodeMap.set(entity.label, existing[0].id);
} else {
  const node = this.graphStore.addNode({ /* ... */ });
  nodeMap.set(entity.label, node.id);
}
```

Because matching is by substring, an entity labeled `"Bot"` matches a pre-existing `"Robot"` or `"Botanist"` node, and `"API"` matches `"APIKey"`. The extractor then attaches the new memory's edges/properties to the wrong node and never creates the correct one.

## Impact

Graph associative memory becomes incorrect: distinct entities whose labels are substrings of one another are silently conflated. Edges (`related_to`, `mentions`, etc.) are wired to the wrong node, properties (including `memoryId`) overwrite an unrelated entity, and subsequent subgraph/context queries return associations the user never expressed. The collision is also order-dependent (`existing[0]`), so results are non-deterministic across runs. This corrupts the data the graph subsystem is built to provide, warranting high severity for a correctness defect at the core of graph memory.

## Required Fix

1. Provide an exact-match lookup for the dedup path (e.g. `findNodesByExactLabel(label)` comparing `n.label.toLowerCase() === normalized`, optionally also matching `type`) and use it in `extractAndStoreGraph`.
2. If substring/fuzzy search is genuinely needed elsewhere, keep it as a separate, clearly named method (e.g. `searchNodesByLabel`) so callers opt in explicitly.
3. Add tests covering substring-colliding labels (e.g. `"Bot"` vs `"Robot"`) to confirm they map to separate nodes.

## References

- CWE-697: Incorrect Comparison — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/697.html

## Description

When a search requests graph enrichment (`includeGraph: true`), `MemoryManager.getGraphContext` looks up seed nodes by feeding the entire user query string into `findNodesByLabel`:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/memory-manager.ts:219-231
private async getGraphContext(query: string): Promise<SubgraphResult | undefined> {
  if (!this.graphStore || !this.graphQuery) return undefined;

  // Find nodes matching the query
  const matchingNodes = this.graphStore.findNodesByLabel(query);
  if (matchingNodes.length === 0) return undefined;

  // Get subgraph around the first matching node
  return this.graphQuery.getSubgraph(matchingNodes[0].id, {
    maxDepth: 2,
    maxResults: 20,
  });
}
```

`findNodesByLabel` matches a node only when the node's label *contains* the argument as a substring (graph-store.ts:89-94). Node labels are short extracted entity names (e.g. `"Database"`, `"Bot"`), whereas `query` is a full natural-language search string (e.g. `"how do I configure the database connection?"`). A short label can never contain the long query as a substring, so `matchingNodes` is empty and the method returns `undefined` for any realistic query.

## Impact

The `graphContext` field of `EnrichedSearchResult` is essentially always `undefined`, so the advertised graph-augmented retrieval silently produces nothing. Callers that opted into `includeGraph` pay the code path's cost but never receive associative context, and the failure is invisible (no error, just an empty result). This makes a headline feature of the memory system non-functional in practice, justifying high severity.

## Required Fix

1. Derive seed labels from the query using the existing `EntityExtractor` (extract entities from the query text, then look each up), instead of matching on the raw query string.
2. Use an exact-label lookup (see the related `findNodesByLabel` substring issue) and iterate over all extracted entities to collect seed nodes, rather than only `matchingNodes[0]`.
3. Add a test that stores content, runs `search(query, { includeGraph: true })` with a natural-language query, and asserts a non-empty `graphContext`.

