- CWE-697: Incorrect Comparison — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/697.html

## Description

When `GraphMaintenance.mergeDuplicates` collapses duplicate nodes, `transferEdges` re-points every edge of the duplicate onto the primary node by calling `addEdge` unconditionally:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/graph-maintenance.ts:178-202
private transferEdges(fromNodeId: string, toNodeId: string): void {
  const outEdges = this.store.getEdgesFrom(fromNodeId);
  for (const edge of outEdges) {
    if (edge.targetId === toNodeId) continue; // Skip self-loops
    this.store.addEdge({
      sourceId: toNodeId,
      targetId: edge.targetId,
      type: edge.type as EdgeType,
      weight: edge.weight,
      properties: edge.properties,
    });
  }
  const inEdges = this.store.getEdgesTo(fromNodeId);
  for (const edge of inEdges) {
    if (edge.sourceId === toNodeId) continue; // Skip self-loops
    this.store.addEdge({ sourceId: edge.sourceId, targetId: toNodeId, /* ... */ });
  }
}
```

`addEdge` (graph-store.ts:144-162) always mints a new edge with a fresh UUID; it never checks for an existing `(sourceId, targetId, type)` triple. So if the primary node already has an edge to the duplicate's neighbor — common, since duplicates are by definition similar nodes with overlapping relationships — the merge produces two parallel edges of the same type between the same pair of nodes.

## Impact

The graph accumulates redundant parallel edges every maintenance cycle. This inflates `edgeCount`, double-counts weights in traversal/ranking (`getNeighbors` sorts by `edge.weight`, BFS sums weights), and slows queries. Because `mergeDuplicates` runs on every `run()` cycle, duplicate edges compound over time, progressively distorting relevance scoring and neighbor ranking. Medium severity: correctness of graph weights/ranking degrades steadily but the graph remains usable.

## Required Fix

1. Before adding a transferred edge, check whether an edge of the same `type` already exists between `sourceId` and `targetId`; if so, merge weights (e.g. take the max or sum-and-cap) and update via `updateEdgeWeight` instead of creating a new edge.
2. Consider adding a `findEdge(sourceId, targetId, type)` helper (or an `addOrUpdateEdge`) to `GraphStore` so de-duplication is centralized and reusable.
3. Add a test that merges two duplicate nodes sharing a neighbor and asserts a single edge (with merged weight) results.

## References

- CWE-694: Use of Multiple Resources with Duplicate Identifier — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/694.html

## Description

On every search, `MemoryManager.search` records an access for each returned entry:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/memory-manager.ts:110-113
// Record access for importance scoring
for (const result of results) {
  this.scorer.recordAccess(result.entry.id);
}
```

but `recordAccess` only bumps an in-memory frequency counter — it does not touch the entry's `accessedAt` timestamp, nor is the entry persisted via `memoryRepository.update`:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/importance-scorer.ts:68-71
recordAccess(memoryId: string): void {
  const current = this.accessCounts.get(memoryId) ?? 0;
  this.accessCounts.set(memoryId, current + 1);
}
```

The recency component of the importance score is computed from `entry.accessedAt` (`computeRecency`, importance-scorer.ts:52,105-109), which is only ever set at store/compaction time and never refreshed on read. So no matter how frequently a memory is retrieved, its `accessedAt` — and therefore its exponential-decay recency score — keeps aging as if the memory were never touched.

## Impact

Retention is driven by `ImportanceScorer.score`, whose `recency` weight (0.3) decays with a 7-day half-life from `accessedAt`. Because reads never refresh `accessedAt`, frequently-accessed-but-old memories decay toward the cold tier and become eviction/compaction candidates (`RetentionPolicy.getEvictionCandidates`) despite being actively used. The frequency signal alone (weight 0.25, in-memory only and lost on restart) cannot compensate. Net effect: the system evicts hot, useful memories and the LRU-style recency logic is effectively dead. Medium severity: degrades retention quality without crashing.

## Required Fix

1. On access, update the entry's `accessedAt` (e.g. `await this.memoryRepository.update(id, { accessedAt: new Date() })`) so the persisted recency reflects reads, in addition to the in-memory frequency counter.
2. Ensure the in-memory access counts are either persisted or rebuilt on startup if they are meant to influence long-term retention.
3. Add a test that searches an entry, then asserts its recency-driven score increases / it is no longer an eviction candidate.

## References

- CWE-670: Always-Incorrect Control Flow Implementation — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/670.html

## Description

`CachedEmbeddingProvider` is documented as an "LRU-style cache" (embedding-provider.ts:13-15), but its eviction is plain FIFO and cache hits do not refresh recency:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/embedding-provider.ts:27-34
async embed(text: string): Promise<number[]> {
  const cached = this.cache.get(text);
  if (cached) return cached;            // hit does NOT re-insert -> no LRU bump
  const embedding = await this.delegate.embed(text);
  this.putCache(text, embedding);
  return embedding;
}

// packages/memory/src/embedding-provider.ts:75-84
private putCache(key: string, value: number[]): void {
  if (this.cache.size >= this.maxCacheSize) {
    // Evict oldest entry (first key in Map iteration order)
    const firstKey = this.cache.keys().next().value;
    if (firstKey !== undefined) this.cache.delete(firstKey);
  }
  this.cache.set(key, value);
}
```

Because `Map` iteration order is insertion order and a hit never re-inserts the key, the entry evicted is always the oldest *inserted*, not the least-recently *used* — i.e. FIFO. Separately, neither `embed`/`embedBatch` nor `putCache` validates that the delegate returned a vector of the expected length (`dimensions()`), so a misbehaving delegate's wrong-sized embeddings are cached and propagated unchecked.

## Impact

Under cache pressure a hot, repeatedly-queried embedding can be evicted while a stale one-shot entry survives, lowering the hit rate and increasing calls to the (often paid / latency-bound) delegate provider. The missing dimension check means a malformed embedding only surfaces later as a `Vector dimension mismatch` throw deep in `cosineSimilarity`/`InMemoryVectorStore.insert`, far from its origin, making diagnosis harder. Medium severity: a performance/robustness defect plus a misleading contract (claims LRU, behaves FIFO).

## Required Fix

1. Make eviction true LRU: on a cache hit, delete and re-`set` the key to move it to the most-recently-used position (Map preserves insertion order), so `putCache` evicts the genuinely least-recently-used entry. Update the comment accordingly, or rename to reflect FIFO if FIFO is intended.
2. Validate embedding length against `this.delegate.dimensions()` in `embed`/`embedBatch` before caching, throwing a clear error at the source if it mismatches.
3. Add tests for eviction order under repeated access and for dimension-mismatch rejection.

## References

- CWE-1188: Insecure Default Initialization of Resource — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/1188.html

## Description

`cosineSimilarity` returns `0` whenever either vector has zero magnitude:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/vector-store.ts:52-53
const magnitude = Math.sqrt(normA) * Math.sqrt(normB);
if (magnitude === 0) return 0;
```

and `InMemoryVectorStore` defaults the similarity threshold to `0.0` (vector-store.ts:69) and keeps only results scoring `>= minScore`:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/vector-store.ts:96-101
const minScore = threshold ?? this.defaultThreshold;
const results: VectorSearchResult[] = [];
for (const entry of this.entries.values()) {
  const score = cosineSimilarity(query, entry.embedding);
  if (score >= minScore) {
    results.push({ id: entry.id, score, metadata: entry.metadata });
  }
}
```

Two coupled problems: (1) a zero vector (e.g. an all-zero or failed embedding) yields similarity `0` against everything, which is indistinguishable from "orthogonal/unrelated" rather than "undefined"; with the default threshold it then passes the `>= 0` filter and pollutes results. (2) With `minScore = 0.0`, any entry whose true cosine similarity is negative (vectors pointing in opposing directions) is silently discarded — these may still be the closest available matches, but they never appear, and a zero-similarity zero-vector outranks them.

## Impact

Search quality is distorted in two ways: genuinely dissimilar (negative-similarity) entries are dropped even when they are the best available candidates for sparse stores, while degenerate zero-vector entries leak into results at score 0 and can outrank legitimate negative-similarity matches. Downstream, hybrid retrieval's reciprocal-rank fusion consumes these mis-ordered/incomplete lists. Medium severity: retrieval relevance is degraded subtly and silently, without errors.

## Required Fix

1. Distinguish the zero-vector case from genuine zero similarity — e.g. treat a zero-magnitude vector as no-match (skip it) or surface it explicitly, rather than returning a comparable `0` score.
2. Reconsider the default threshold: use a small negative sentinel (e.g. `-Infinity`/`-1`) as the "no filtering" default so negative-similarity matches are retained when no explicit threshold is set, and document the chosen semantics.
3. Add tests covering a zero query/entry vector and a query that only has negative-similarity matches.

## References

- CWE-682: Incorrect Calculation — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/682.html

## Description

`InMemoryVectorStore` and `InMemoryGraphStore` accumulate entries in unbounded `Map`s with no eviction policy or size cap:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/vector-store.ts:62-79
export class InMemoryVectorStore implements VectorStore {
  private readonly entries = new Map<string, VectorEntry>();
  // ...
  async insert(entry: VectorEntry): Promise<void> {
    if (entry.embedding.length !== this.dimensions) { /* throw */ }
    this.entries.set(entry.id, entry);   // grows without bound
  }
}
```

`InMemoryGraphStore` (graph-store.ts:62-79) likewise grows its `nodes`/`edges`/`outEdges`/`inEdges` maps without any cap. Worse, when `MemoryCompaction.compact` retires cold memories it deletes them from the repository only — it never removes their vectors from the index:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/compaction.ts:97-101
// Delete the original entries
for (const entry of group) {
  await this.memoryRepository.delete(entry.id);   // repository only
  result.deletedIds.push(entry.id);
}
```

There is no corresponding `vectorStore.delete(entry.id)` / `semanticSearch.removeFromIndex(entry.id)` call (compare `MemoryManager.forget`, memory-manager.ts:148-151, which does remove from the index). So every compaction leaves orphaned vectors behind.

## Impact

Memory usage of the in-memory stores grows monotonically with ingest and is never reclaimed by compaction, the very mechanism meant to bound it. Orphaned vector entries also corrupt search: `SemanticSearch.resolveResults` (semantic-search.ts:112-124) drops a hit when `memoryRepository.findById` returns null, so an orphan silently consumes a top-K slot, displacing a real result and shrinking the returned set. Over a long-running process this is a steady memory leak plus degrading recall. Medium severity: unbounded growth and silent recall loss in long-lived deployments.

## Required Fix

1. In the compaction path, after deleting an entry from the repository, also remove it from the vector index (e.g. `await this.semanticSearch.removeFromIndex(entry.id)` / `vectorStore.delete(entry.id)`), and remove any graph nodes/edges keyed to that `memoryId`.
2. Give `InMemoryVectorStore`/`InMemoryGraphStore` an optional max-size with an eviction policy (e.g. evict lowest-importance or oldest), or document that they are dev-only and must be backed by a bounded persistent store in production.
3. Add a test that compacts entries and asserts the vector store size shrinks accordingly (no orphans).

## References

- CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html

## Description

`SemanticSearch.indexBatch` builds the list of texts needing embeddings, then resolves each entry's embedding by calling `indexOf` on that list inside a `.map` over all entries:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/semantic-search.ts:48-73
async indexBatch(entries: MemoryEntry[]): Promise<void> {
  const textsToEmbed: string[] = [];
  const indicesToEmbed: number[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
    if (!entries[i].embedding) {
      textsToEmbed.push(entries[i].content);
      indicesToEmbed.push(i);
    }
  }
  let newEmbeddings: number[][] = [];
  if (textsToEmbed.length > 0) {
    newEmbeddings = await this.embeddingProvider.embedBatch(textsToEmbed);
  }
  const vectorEntries = entries.map((entry, i) => {
    const embeddingIdx = indicesToEmbed.indexOf(i);   // O(n) inside O(n) map
    const embedding = entry.embedding ?? newEmbeddings[embeddingIdx];
    return { id: entry.id, embedding, metadata: { tags: entry.tags } };
  });
  await this.vectorStore.insertBatch(vectorEntries);
}
```

`indicesToEmbed.indexOf(i)` is an O(n) scan executed once per entry, making the resolution step O(n^2) in the number of entries.

## Impact

For large batches (e.g. bulk import / re-indexing thousands of memories) the quadratic scan dominates and wastes CPU, even though the underlying data already permits O(n). No correctness issue and small batches are unaffected, hence low severity. (Note: when an entry has no embedding and is not in `indicesToEmbed`, `indexOf` returns `-1` and `newEmbeddings[-1]` is `undefined`; in the current call paths every embedding-less entry is added to `indicesToEmbed`, so this does not misfire today, but the lookup is fragile as well as slow.)

## Required Fix

1. Replace the positional `indexOf` with a direct mapping: iterate `indicesToEmbed`/`newEmbeddings` together and assign `results[idx] = newEmbeddings[j]` (mirroring the pattern already used in `CachedEmbeddingProvider.embedBatch`), or build a `Map<number, number[]>` from original index to embedding for O(1) lookup.
2. Add a test/benchmark over a large batch to guard against regressions.

## References

- CWE-407: Inefficient Algorithmic Complexity — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/407.html

## Description

`PatternEntityExtractor` identifies entities only via a capitalized-ASCII-word regex, then relates every extracted pair:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/entity-extractor.ts:44-57
const capitalizedPattern = /\b([A-Z][a-z]+(?:\s+[A-Z][a-z]+)*)\b/g;
let match;
while ((match = capitalizedPattern.exec(text)) !== null) {
  const label = match[1];
  if (!seen.has(label.toLowerCase()) && label.length > 1) {
    seen.add(label.toLowerCase());
    entities.push({ label, type: "entity", properties: { source: "pattern" } });
  }
}

// packages/memory/src/entity-extractor.ts:73-83
// Create "related_to" relations between co-occurring entities
for (let i = 0; i < entities.length; i++) {
  for (let j = i + 1; j < entities.length; j++) {
    relations.push({ sourceLabel: entities[i].label, targetLabel: entities[j].label, type: "related_to", weight: 0.5 });
  }
}
```

The pattern `[A-Z][a-z]+...` requires an uppercase letter followed by lowercase letters, so it misses all-caps acronyms (`API`, `SQL`, `HTTP`, `USA`), single-letter-plus-digit tokens, and any non-ASCII/Unicode names (Cyrillic, accented Latin, CJK). The relation step then connects all entity pairs, which is O(n^2) edges for n entities in one memory.

## Impact

Entity recall is poor for technical and non-English content — acronyms and Unicode names (relevant to this project, which handles Russian text) are never captured, so the graph omits important nodes. Meanwhile the all-pairs relation builder produces a dense O(n^2) edge set per memory, inflating the graph with low-signal `related_to` edges (weight 0.5) that dilute meaningful relationships and slow traversal. Low severity: this is the documented heuristic fallback (`LLMEntityExtractor` is the primary path), but it materially degrades graph quality when used.

## Required Fix

1. Broaden the entity pattern to include acronyms and Unicode letters (e.g. allow all-caps tokens and use Unicode-aware classes / the `u` flag), while still filtering stopwords.
2. Replace the dense all-pairs relation construction with a bounded heuristic (e.g. relate only entities within a sentence/window, or cap the number of relations per memory) to avoid O(n^2) edge explosion.
3. Add tests covering acronyms (`API`), Unicode names, and an entity-dense input asserting a bounded relation count.

## References

- CWE-407: Inefficient Algorithmic Complexity — https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/407.html

## Description

`InMemoryGraphStore.getNode` is a read accessor that mutates the node it returns by stamping `accessedAt` on every call:

```ts
// packages/memory/src/graph-store.ts:81-87
getNode(id: string): GraphNode | undefined {
  const node = this.nodes.get(id);
  if (node) {
    node.accessedAt = new Date();
  }
  return node;
}
```

Because `getNode` returns the stored object by reference, the timestamp is updated in place. `GraphQuery` calls `getNode` heavily during traversal — `bfs` (graph-query.ts:49), `getNeighbors` (graph-query.ts:153,164), and `reconstructPath` (graph-query.ts:185,196) — so a single subgraph or path query silently refreshes `accessedAt` on every node it visits, not just nodes the user explicitly fetched. This also makes simple reads non-idempotent. It is inconsistent with the document-store path, where `MemoryManager.search` deliberately routes access tracking through `scorer.recordAccess` and does not refresh `MemoryEntry.accessedAt` at all (see the related `search`/`accessedAt` finding).

## Impact

`accessedAt` is the staleness signal for `GraphMaintenance.pruneStaleNodes` (graph-maintenance.ts:157) and the tie-breaker for which node survives in `mergeDuplicates` (graph-maintenance.ts:88). Because traversal touches transit nodes, internal/background queries keep nodes "fresh" purely as a side effect of being on a path, defeating stale-node pruning and skewing merge decisions toward whatever was traversed most recently rather than genuinely accessed. The two memory subsystems also disagree on whether reads update recency, making behavior hard to reason about. Low severity: subtle skew in maintenance heuristics, no crash or data loss.

## Required Fix

1. Decide on a single, explicit access-tracking policy and apply it consistently across graph and document memory. Either (a) make `getNode` a pure read and update `accessedAt` only through an explicit `touch(id)` / `recordAccess` call from genuine user-facing accesses, or (b) have traversal use a non-mutating internal lookup so transit nodes are not refreshed.
2. Document the intended semantics of `accessedAt` (what counts as an access) on `GraphStore`.
3. Add a test asserting that a read-only traversal does not change `accessedAt` of intermediate nodes (per the chosen policy).

## References

- CWE-707: Improper Neutralization (side-effecting accessor) — see general guidance; primarily a design/consistency defect.
